<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>False</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1605</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>rulealize</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>Rulealize.Cli</PackageId>
    <Version>0.10.0</Version>
    <Authors>Reny</Authors>
    <Description>Runs a Rulealize rule set from a command line: restore the plugins it requires, report what a plugin folder provides, and check, list the legal inputs, apply one, or walk it interactively — a rule set that holds other rule sets included.</Description>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/reny-develop/Rulealize.Cli</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The runtime, and every command here is a caller of it: restore and check compile a
    document, moves asks what is legal, apply and play transition a state and ask what could
    happen to it.

    PluginRequirement and PluginResolution come from the same package on purpose. Both are
    pure and both could have been written here, and the reason they were not is that a
    second reading of ^1.0 would eventually differ from the runtime's, and the way it would
    show is this tool assembling a folder the runtime then refuses.

    The same holds one kind of requirement over, and 0.8.0 is where it stopped being a gap.
    RuleSetRequirement.ReadFrom says what a document holds; Choose says which version of it
    to take, so that "the lowest satisfying version wins" is stated once rather than once per
    resolver; and RuleSetIdentity.ReadFrom says what arrived, without compiling it, which is
    what a fetcher can afford while holding one document and none of the rest. Every one of
    them could have been written here, and every one of them would have drifted.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Rulealize" Version="0.8.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
